WEBVTT


00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.530
Where do you get patterns
for your machine knitting?

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:10.770
Once upon a time, there were beautiful
glossy magazines and hardbound books

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.870
written just for machine knitters.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:19.350
Sadly, that's not the case any longer, but
there are options that we can use to get

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.030
beautiful knitting patterns.

00:00:22.259 --> 00:00:28.770
Let's take a look at some of those options
and make some choices as to what method

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:29.970
works best for you.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.530
First, there are vintage patterns.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:38.180
There's a ton of vintage
patterns available for us to use.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:44.960
The problem with these vintage patterns
are that they're written for yarn that no

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.160
longer exists.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:49.300
Often they're very small sizes.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.340
The designs are pretty dated.

00:00:53.269 --> 00:00:57.769
The good news with these patterns is they
have some really unusual stitch patterns

00:00:58.085 --> 00:01:00.065
and innovative techniques.

00:01:00.275 --> 00:01:06.604
So there's a lot to be gained by at least
exploring and examining these vintage

00:01:06.604 --> 00:01:07.295
patterns.

00:01:09.005 --> 00:01:10.445
Can you use these patterns?

00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:11.435
Well, of course.

00:01:11.705 --> 00:01:16.955
You'll need to do some math to convert the
stitch gauge, and you might want to update

00:01:16.955 --> 00:01:22.085
the styles just a little bit, but these
are definitely an option for us to use.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.490
Another choice is to use
hand knitting patterns.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:34.050
Now we've got updated styles and a wealth
of beautiful designs by modern designers.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.990
These also come in larger sizes.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.490
Again, though, you're going to have to
match the designer's gauge, and if you

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.840
choose not to use the yarn that the
designer used, you'll need to do some math

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.340
to convert your gauge and
your yarn to their design.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:56.420
Another issue with hand knitting patterns
to keep in mind is many stitch patterns

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.410
that are designed for hand knitting
are not appropriate for our machines.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.260
You know, think of terms
of knit and purl stitches.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:07.310
So you will need to
substitute stitch patterns.

00:02:08.255 --> 00:02:11.295
Another thing of wha, the hand knitting
patterns, the modern hand knitting

00:02:11.295 --> 00:02:11.865
patterns.

00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:17.805
So many of them are written to be knit
from the top down or be knit in the round.

00:02:18.195 --> 00:02:21.945
Now on our flatbed knitting
machines, that really isn't possible.

00:02:22.335 --> 00:02:27.715
Can you take a top down pattern and just
knit a front and a back and knit from the

00:02:27.734 --> 00:02:29.325
bottom up or from the top down?

00:02:29.325 --> 00:02:32.954
Either way, knit it flat
in other words, absolutely.

00:02:33.225 --> 00:02:34.995
Again, there's some math involved.

00:02:35.345 --> 00:02:37.625
And you'll need to match the gauge.

00:02:38.135 --> 00:02:41.945
I'm going to throw in a little
commercial here for Knit it Now Patterns.

00:02:42.274 --> 00:02:46.054
With our Dynamic Patterns there
is no software to install.

00:02:46.054 --> 00:02:47.674
You don't have to do any math.

00:02:47.885 --> 00:02:49.024
Three steps.

00:02:49.114 --> 00:02:56.345
You choose a size, you enter your gauge
and your stitch pattern and just get

00:02:56.345 --> 00:02:57.005
knitting.

00:02:57.575 --> 00:03:02.885
So checkout and KnititNOW.com/patterns
for our dynamic.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.660
Perfect Fit patterns.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.000
And I'll talk more about
them in just a minute.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:12.220
Now another way to get knitting patterns
for machine knitters is to use pencil and

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:12.700
paper.

00:03:13.609 --> 00:03:15.369
You could chart your own patterns.

00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:19.420
You know, once upon a time you could go
down to the local yarn store and someone

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:22.170
in the local yard store would
chart a pattern for you.

00:03:23.179 --> 00:03:24.434
Those days are over.

00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:28.454
I think, and many of us
don't know how to chart.

00:03:28.845 --> 00:03:31.964
It is something that is a skill
that as a machine knitter.

00:03:31.964 --> 00:03:35.325
You should have, you should
know the basics of charting.

00:03:35.774 --> 00:03:39.225
If nothing else, you're not going to
sit down with a blank piece of paper and

00:03:39.225 --> 00:03:44.864
charter a sweater for yourself, but you
might want to adjust the a sleeve length

00:03:44.984 --> 00:03:47.295
or make a minor change to a pattern.

00:03:47.565 --> 00:03:51.855
Knowing how to chart is really
going to help you in that area.

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:56.745
Speaking of charting, using
software is much more common today.

00:03:56.984 --> 00:04:03.345
There are quite a few software packages
out there that you can use to learn how to

00:04:03.345 --> 00:04:05.595
chart, to chart your own sweaters.

00:04:06.555 --> 00:04:09.674
One of the issues with
any software is the cost.

00:04:09.674 --> 00:04:10.965
You have to purchase the thing.

00:04:10.965 --> 00:04:11.325
You have to

00:04:11.325 --> 00:04:12.425
install the thing.

00:04:12.425 --> 00:04:13.925
You have to maintain the thing.

00:04:13.925 --> 00:04:15.545
You have to learn the thing.

00:04:15.785 --> 00:04:19.295
And the learning curve on some of
these software packages is pretty big.

00:04:19.685 --> 00:04:24.125
I don't know about you, but I'd rather
speed knitting than sitting at my computer

00:04:24.125 --> 00:04:25.325
playing with software.

00:04:25.895 --> 00:04:32.735
But if software and original design is in
your future, you might want to consider

00:04:32.735 --> 00:04:34.505
some of these software packages.

00:04:35.255 --> 00:04:39.700
The nice thing about software is you can
come up with a design that you can use

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:46.450
over and over, change the gauge and make
minor changes to it and have really a lot

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:49.900
of control over your knitting pattern.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:55.659
Now, something to consider when you're
considering software is the output of that

00:04:55.659 --> 00:04:56.349
software.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.510
What's the written pattern

00:04:59.475 --> 00:05:00.255
look like?

00:05:00.585 --> 00:05:03.585
Whether you print it out on paper
or you view it on the computer.

00:05:04.005 --> 00:05:08.445
You need to be comfortable with
how the output of that pattern is.

00:05:08.445 --> 00:05:11.205
So something to consider when
you're shopping for software.

00:05:12.495 --> 00:05:16.965
Another way of getting knitting patterns
for machine knitting is to use a charting

00:05:16.965 --> 00:05:17.475
device.

00:05:17.955 --> 00:05:19.605
Now, what's a charting device?

00:05:19.935 --> 00:05:23.115
It's something that looks
like, well, this is one style.

00:05:23.115 --> 00:05:25.155
They came in a lot of different styles.

00:05:25.365 --> 00:05:28.905
Some of the charting devices are
built right into the machine.

00:05:28.935 --> 00:05:36.075
Others are a separate device like this,
and they are a visual way of knitting.

00:05:36.585 --> 00:05:41.775
You draw the shape of your garment piece,
whether it's a sleeve or a front or a

00:05:41.775 --> 00:05:42.165
back.

00:05:42.165 --> 00:05:44.985
You draw it on a piece of paper or a card.

00:05:44.985 --> 00:05:45.885
What a specific.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:47.370
Piece of paper.

00:05:47.849 --> 00:05:54.870
And then as you knit and as you push the
carriage back and forth, that design, that

00:05:54.870 --> 00:06:02.310
drawing advances, you follow the line and
do your increases and decreases to shape

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:08.835
your garment piece based on the visual
little image that you're looking at.

00:06:09.165 --> 00:06:12.555
Now, if you're lucky enough to have a
charting device for your knitting machine

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:16.275
and you haven't tried it, I
really encourage you to try it.

00:06:16.545 --> 00:06:19.785
This method is very popular
with a lot of knitters.

00:06:20.175 --> 00:06:20.745
Okay?

00:06:20.745 --> 00:06:26.505
I'm biased, but using Knit it Now Dynamic
Patterns is the best way to get knitting

00:06:26.505 --> 00:06:27.195
patterns.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.160
You don't have to match my gauge.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:34.270
You can use your gauge, your
stitch pattern, any machine.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:41.200
We also have a perfect fit feature so that
you can fine-tune the fit and the size of

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:41.890
your knitting.

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:47.680
The patterns have no abbreviation, so none
of that cryptic abbreviation stuff like in

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.240
the old vintage patterns.

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:55.840
We also include tips and tricks and
instruction right in the pattern.

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:59.790
Videos are embedded in many
of the patterns as well.

00:07:02.175 --> 00:07:05.895
I invite you to go to
KnititNOW.com/patterns.

00:07:06.135 --> 00:07:10.455
And take a look at our perfect
fit dynamic blueprints.

00:07:11.685 --> 00:07:17.775
No matter what method you choose, I really
suggest that you pick one of the methods

00:07:17.955 --> 00:07:19.155
and use it.

00:07:20.175 --> 00:07:25.185
Don't spend your precious knitting time
learning software and translating vintage

00:07:25.185 --> 00:07:28.995
patterns and doing a bunch of
math with hand knitting patterns.

00:07:30.015 --> 00:07:32.385
Pick one method and stick with it.

00:07:32.835 --> 00:07:35.085
You'll find you'll have much more success.

00:07:36.735 --> 00:07:40.755
Until next time, thanks for
watching and happy knitting.

